# RFC #362 desktop.13 (Windows distribution patch, also valid upstream): B2.
#
# An Agent run no longer writes under the window's lease.
#
# A reply used to save and write its project changes with the lease of the
# window that started it (X-OpenDirector-Session). Switching projects in that
# window released the lease, so the reply's own writes were refused with
# "工程已关闭或会话已过期" while the reply still reported complete (a silent
# loss). Stop and the reply viewer also required the window's lease, so Stop
# failed from any window that had moved on. A 60 s lapse of the window's
# heartbeat (a minimised or throttled window) had the same effect.
#
# The fix is a run-scoped lease. The window's lease is checked once when the
# turn starts; the run then takes its own lease row, owned by that window
# (sessions.owner), renews it while it streams, and releases it before the
# end frame is published. Tool writes, conversation saves, and the final
# save all use the run lease.
#
# Lease semantics for everyone else are unchanged or stricter: another window
# still gets project_open_elsewhere while a run holds the project, rename and
# delete stay blocked, and the window that started the run may reopen its
# project while the run goes on. Stop and the reply viewer only need the
# running turn, so they no longer depend on the window lease. Existing
# catalogs gain the owner column on first open.
